Well, yes indeed.

And last year's tournament highlight was the player
(name forgotten, but may it be graven in bronze for
NetHack-eternity) who made the point for all time by
rolling the 31 bit score counter then deliberately
dying somewhere in the Astral Planes at the vicious
jaws of a deranged newt.

High scores are thus proved simply _meaningless_,
end of story, and bear little-to-no relation to
playing skill or achievements in a particular game.

So a better scoring system, difficult to create and
to bring to consensus as it might be, is a very
worthwhile goal, and a worthy thread for discussion.

>From the contributions so far, there are going to be
some _severe_ disagreements as to what constitutes
score-worthy game actions.

One current proposal, "securing the castle wand of
wishing" for example, seems to me wrongheaded.

By the time I can get to the castle, a rare thing
indeed, that wand of wishing is to me merely a tool
of game abuse, I should be easily able to ascend
wishless if I'm already kitted up enough to survive
to the castle level.

[Everybody has personal conducts: Raisse doesn't use
amulets of life saving, I despise wishes and
genocides as weaknesses in my already weak play, I
usually discard genocide scrolls and wishing devices
unused, though the occasional wish for a fixed +2
SDSM in the very early game just so I can experience
some of the later game still overcomes me once in a
while. I haven't uncursed-genocided anything in a
couple or three years of play, though, and it's
likely been longer than that since I used more than
a single wish in a game.]

The real accomplishment is not grabbing the wand,
but is instead getting into and out of the Valley of
the Dead via the Castle.

A subordinate accomplishment, one I've never done,
is lowering the <swearwords> drawbridge; I _always_
go in the back way, because I can never manage to
simultaneously get a musical instrument, and get to
the castle level.

My play isn't nearly that organized yet, which lack
of organization is one of the several of my
personality disorders I use NetHack to practice
trying to overcome.

